## Suspect checks (probe to rewrite, not product failures)

- c1735 (R64): witness-r64-r1 reports the probe wrong: The probe adds a precondition neither clause states: it fails a clause when `getKeybindings().getKeys("app.session.delete")` does not include "backspace". R64.1 says only "With the default keymap, Backspace (\x7f) on a non-empty query edits it (query "ga" becomes "g") and does not request delete confirmation", and R64.2 only "With the default keymap, Backspace on an empty query requests delete confirmation (confirm.act === "delete-confirm")" — neither says anything about which keys app.session.delete is bound to. On the left tree the default keymap binds app.session.delete to ["ctrl+d"], yet Backspace on "ga" still edits the query to "g" with no delete confirmation and Backspace on an empty query still requests delete confirmation, so both clauses hold there; the check discriminates only through the extra binding assertion, not through the behaviour the clauses describe.
- c1922 (X24): clauses-x24-r1 reports the probe wrong: The probe's own boot stub is at fault, not the product: it serves @oh-my-pi/pi-natives with a FileLock whose tryAcquire() answers undefined, and upstream's session storage (right tree, session-storage.ts #acquireOsPublishLock) requires a handle, so the probe's session write throws before the behaviour is observed. Its right-side failure is therefore setup trouble, not the clause's behaviour ("Durability: the behaviour still holds after the product's own clean restart ... and after its persistence rewrite"). The rewritten probe x24d_7_durability.ts (new file, assertions declared from the clause text, kept as c1927) uses a FileLock stub that reports ownership, so both parents fail for the real reason (left: the four select fields absent; right: "hud" absent) and it passes on merged.

